* ============================================================================
* workspace_minidb_multi_shakedown.dts
*
* A LONG, SELF-CONTAINED SHAKEDOWN OF TWO LANES AT ONCE: the multi-workspace
* tree (AIF-078) and the memo-resident MINIDB container (AIF-070). Written
* 2026-08-28 against build 48273f28.
*
* WHY BOTH IN ONE FILE. They are not independent. A MINIDB container carries a
* POSTURE, a posture is a picture of a WORKSPACE, and the interesting failures
* live where the two meet -- a container hydrated into RAM lands its tables in
* whatever workspace is current, at slots the posture does not control. A
* script that exercised either alone would miss that seam.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HOW TO READ THIS SCRIPT -- four rules, each of them paid for
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*
* 1. COUNT THE MARKERS. 37 must PRINT -- 13 guards and 24 arms. An errored
*    marker PRINTS NOTHING rather than going red, so a full screen of .T.
*    with 36 lines is a FAILURE, not a pass. The count is the check.
*
*    THIS NUMBER HAS BEEN WRONG ONCE AND STALE ONCE, both recorded rather
*    than quietly fixed. The first draft said 34 by miscounting; the first
*    RUN then added two guards and left it reading 35. A hand-maintained
*    count beside the thing it counts is a SECOND DECLARATION and it drifts
*    exactly the way the WORKSPACE usage header drifted three times. Derive
*    it, never trust it:  grep -c '^? "MWX_'
*
*    THIS NUMBER WAS WRONG IN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS FILE and is corrected
*    here rather than quietly fixed. It read 34, from arithmetic that counted
*    MWX_G0a-d as one guard and then added one back. The author of a read
*    rule about counting miscounted the thing the rule counts. It is left on
*    the record because the correction is the same lesson the rule teaches:
*    do not trust a number you did not derive from the artifact. The
*    derivation is `grep -c '^? "MWX_'`.
*
* 2. GUARDS FIRST, ARMS SECOND. Every MWX_G* proves an INPUT. If any guard
*    reads .F., treat every MWX_T* below it as UNPROVEN rather than as
*    failing -- a fixture failure wearing a verb failure's clothes is the
*    exact trap WSL_G4/G5 were added to close.
*
* 3. NOTHING HERE ASSERTS THAT AN AREA IS EMPTY. No marker in this language
*    can (USE_AGAIN, three cuts): the marker evaluator binds a null area
*    whether or not it is open, so a comparison over a CLOSED area does not
*    error -- it PRINTS A VERDICT, and the verdict depends on the OPERATOR.
*    Measured 2026-08-28: (SID >= 1) reads .T. over a closed area while
*    (SO_ID = 6) reads .F. Absence is therefore proven by CONTRAST and by
*    reading the transcript, never by an arm.
*
* 4. ASSERTIONS ADDRESS BY NAME, NEVER BY SLOT, after any posture LOAD.
*    R130: a posture's AREA numbers are KEYS, not addresses, and the load
*    prints so. Tables land at 0..N-1 regardless of what the posture said.
*    Four specs carried slot-addressed arms into 2026-08-28 and produced
*    FALSE GREENS the moment their fixtures shrank.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WHAT IT COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*
* This script MINTS DURABLE CATALOG ROWS. WORKSPACE NEW writes a birth row
* (D10.1), WORKSPACE OPEN <dir> AS <name> mints one too, and every SAVE
* appends to a name's chain. Teardown DESTROYs everything it declares, so no
* NAME keeps a live row and a second run mints fresh rather than ADOPTING --
* but the ROWS remain, because D10.3 retirement is SUPERSESSION, not deletion.
* That is the history the design keeps.
*
* RUN IT BRACKETED IF YOU CARE ABOUT THAT: registered under a spec flagged
* mints_catalog, REGRESSION points the WORKSPACES slot at a per-run scratch
* root and production is untouched. Run bare and the rows land in production.
*
* Fixtures live in DBF/SANDBOX and are erased at both ends. Nothing outside
* SANDBOX and the workspace catalog is written.
*
* Run: DO workspace_minidb_multi_shakedown
* ============================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

FORMULA "MWX-SHAKEDOWN-BEGIN"

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 0 -- PRE-CLEAN.
*
* ORDER IS THE ARGUMENT: DESTROY comes BEFORE ERASE because retiring a name
* needs the row it is retiring. On a clean start every line here prints
* "no such workspace" and changes nothing; on a dirty one it is the
* difference between minting and ADOPTING a previous run's identity.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXKID
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXTWO
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXAS
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXDB

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
ERASE MWXP CONFIRM
ERASE MWXSHARE CONFIRM
ERASE MWXK CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
ERASE MWXQ CONFIRM
ERASE MWXSHARE CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
ERASE MWXNOTE CONFIRM

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 1 -- FIXTURES.
*
* Two directories, each with a table of its own, PLUS a table of the SAME
* NAME in both. The shared name is not decoration: one file open in several
* workspaces is the case that separates a per-workspace registry from a
* global one, and it is what MWX_T5 turns on.
* ===========================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
SELECT 0
CREATE X64 MWXP (PK C(4), PLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE PK WITH "A1"
REPLACE PLBL WITH "ALPHA_ONE"
APPEND
REPLACE PK WITH "A2"
REPLACE PLBL WITH "ALPHA_TWO"

SELECT 1
CREATE X64 MWXSHARE (SK C(4), SLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE SK WITH "S1"
REPLACE SLBL WITH "SHARED_IN_A"

&& MWXK EXISTS SO THE CHILD WORKSPACE CAN HOLD A TABLE WHOSE NAME IS UNIQUE.
&& The first cut had the child open MWXSHARE, which exists in BOTH fixture
&& directories -- so the arm reading it resolved to whichever copy won
&& first-wins (R112) and asserted against the wrong table. The ambiguity
&& ledger is exercised deliberately by T5; it must not leak into an arm that
&& is about something else.
SELECT 4
CREATE X64 MWXK (KK C(4), KLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE KK WITH "K1"
REPLACE KLBL WITH "CHILD_TABLE"

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
SELECT 2
CREATE X64 MWXQ (QK C(4), QLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE QK WITH "B1"
REPLACE QLBL WITH "BETA_ONE"

SELECT 3
CREATE X64 MWXSHARE (SK C(4), SLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE SK WITH "S1"
REPLACE SLBL WITH "SHARED_IN_B"

SELECT 0
GO 2
? "MWX_G0a_fixture_A_parent_written:" + (ALLTRIM(PLBL) = "ALPHA_TWO")
SELECT 1
GO 1
? "MWX_G0b_fixture_A_shared_written:" + (ALLTRIM(SLBL) = "SHARED_IN_A")
SELECT 2
GO 1
? "MWX_G0c_fixture_B_parent_written:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")
SELECT 3
GO 1
? "MWX_G0d_fixture_B_shared_written:" + (ALLTRIM(SLBL) = "SHARED_IN_B")
SELECT 4
GO 1
? "MWX_G0e_child_table_written:" + (ALLTRIM(KLBL) = "CHILD_TABLE")

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 2 -- THE TREE: NEW, SWITCH, UNDER, AND MEMBERSHIP.
*
* An area joins whichever workspace is CURRENT when the area is OPENED, so
* the model is SWITCH-then-open and never open-then-assign. These arms read
* that by opening into different workspaces and then asking each one what it
* still holds.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE NEW MWXONE
WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXONE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
WORKSPACE ADD MWXP.dbf

WORKSPACE NEW MWXTWO
WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXTWO
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
WORKSPACE ADD MWXQ.dbf

WORKSPACE NEW MWXKID UNDER MWXONE
WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXKID
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
WORKSPACE ADD MWXK.dbf

WORKSPACE REGISTRY

SELECT MWXP
GO 1
? "MWX_G1a_one_holds_its_area:" + (ALLTRIM(PLBL) = "ALPHA_ONE")
SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
? "MWX_G1b_two_holds_its_area:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")
SELECT MWXK
GO 1
? "MWX_G1c_child_holds_its_area:" + (ALLTRIM(KLBL) = "CHILD_TABLE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T1 -- OPEN ... AS <name> OVERRIDES THE DIRECTORY LEAF (R128).
*
* A directory open names the workspace after the leaf. Two directories with
* the same leaf would ask for one handle; AS is the way out. It is also a
* MINTING form -- it births a catalog row like NEW does -- which is why this
* script retires MWXAS in teardown even though no NEW created it.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB AS MWXAS
SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
? "MWX_T1_open_as_named_the_workspace:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T2 -- ADDITIVE RE-ENTRY LEAVES OPEN TABLES ALONE (R128).
*
* Re-entering a workspace adds only what is not already open. The arm parks
* the cursor on a NON-DEFAULT row first: if re-entry had re-opened the table
* the cursor would be back at row 1, so the assertion is that the PARK
* SURVIVED, which a re-open cannot fake.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB AS MWXAS
SELECT MWXQ
? "MWX_T2_reentry_left_the_open_table_alone:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T3 / T4 -- SCOPED CLOSE, AND THE FLAG THAT DECIDES WHETHER IT DESCENDS.
*
* A bare CLOSE is scoped to the CURRENT workspace. With one workspace open
* this is byte-identical to the old sweep; with two it is the difference
* between closing yours and closing someone else's. T3 is that discriminator:
* MWXTWO is a SIBLING and its area must still read after MWXONE closes.
*
* T4 IS ABOUT SET RECURSION, AND IT WAS WRONG IN THE FIRST CUT. It asserted
* that a NESTED workspace's area survives a parent's close -- under the
* DEFAULT, which is RECURSION ON, and ON means the close DESCENDS. The engine
* did exactly what cmd_workspace.cpp documents ("2 workspace(s) closed") and
* the arm called it a failure. A spec that contradicts the contract is a spec
* defect, not a finding.
*
* Rewritten to test the FLAG rather than the default: under OFF the workspaces
* are PARALLEL, so the child's area must survive. The ON case is proven by
* CONTRAST and read from the transcript -- "N workspace(s) closed" -- because
* asserting that the child WAS closed would be asserting absence, and no
* marker in this language can do that.
*
* RECURSION IS RESTORED TO ON immediately afterwards. A spec that leaves a
* session setting flipped poisons everything downstream of it; ERRORSTOP is
* the cautionary precedent.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SET RECURSION OFF
WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXONE
WORKSPACE CLOSE

SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
? "MWX_T3_sibling_workspace_survived_a_scoped_close:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")
SELECT MWXK
GO 1
? "MWX_T4_recursion_off_left_the_nested_workspace_open:" + (ALLTRIM(KLBL) = "CHILD_TABLE")
SET RECURSION ON

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T5 -- ONE FILE, TWO WORKSPACES, TWO HANDLES.
*
* MWXSHARE exists in BOTH directories under the SAME NAME. This is the case
* a global registry cannot represent. Opening B's copy while A's is already
* open must yield a SECOND handle rather than replacing the first, and the
* labels differ so the arm can tell which one it is reading.
*
* NOTE THE AMBIGUITY LINE IN THE TRANSCRIPT: with two same-named tables open,
* name resolution reports "open in 2 areas ... resolved to area N, first-wins
* is a migration step (R112)". That is the ledger doing its job, not an
* error, and it is why T5 reads through the CURRENT area after an explicit
* SELECT rather than trusting the bare name.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXTWO
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
WORKSPACE ADD MWXSHARE.dbf
GO 1
? "MWX_T5_second_copy_opened_beside_the_first:" + (ALLTRIM(SLBL) = "SHARED_IN_B")

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 3 -- THE IDENTITY LADDER: DESTROY, DELETE, AND THE HIGH-WATER MARK.
*
* Retirement and removal are two different verbs and the difference is the
* whole design. This section reads the CATALOG TABLE, not the console:
* WORKSPACES.dbf is an ordinary x64 table -- the map drawn in the same ink as
* the territory -- so the arms compare FIELDS, per the FIELDMGR_APPEND
* doctrine that a spec asserting SHAPE passes green on a blanked table.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T6 -- DESTROY REFUSES A WORKSPACE THAT STILL HOLDS AREAS, and the area
* survives. It does not cascade, so it can never be the thing that silently
* orphaned an open area. The arm asks the answerable question: did the KNOWN
* OCCUPANT survive the refusal.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXTWO
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
WORKSPACE ADD MWXQ.dbf
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXTWO
SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
? "MWX_T6_destroy_refused_and_the_area_survived:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T7 -- DESTROY REFUSES DEFAULT. Invariant I1 needs DEFAULT to outlive
* every other workspace. Again read by survival, not by absence.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY DEFAULT
SELECT MWXQ
GO 1
? "MWX_T7_default_refused_destruction_and_still_works:" + (ALLTRIM(QLBL) = "BETA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T8 -- DESTROY REFUSES A PARENT THAT STILL HAS A CHILD. Destroy the
* child first; the verb refuses rather than cascading.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
SELECT 0
USE MWXP
GO 1
? "MWX_T8_parent_with_a_child_refused_and_disk_intact:" + (ALLTRIM(PLBL) = "ALPHA_ONE")
CLOSE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T9 -- THE LADDER DISCRIMINATOR, and it is one line of meaning:
* destroy a name, create it again, and the SECOND WS_ID MUST BE GREATER.
*
* That can only pass if retirement actually REACHED THE CATALOG. If DESTROY
* silently no-ops, supersedes the wrong row, or writes a flag that does not
* stick, the name still has a live chain, the second NEW ADOPTS it, and the
* two ids are EQUAL. It separates a real retirement from a cheerful message.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXKID
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE

WORKSPACE NEW MWXONE
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR mwx_id1 = WS_ID
? "MWX_G2_first_mint_wrote_a_birth_row:" + ($mwx_id1 > 0)
CLOSE

WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
WORKSPACE NEW MWXONE
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR mwx_id2 = WS_ID
? "MWX_T9_remint_after_destroy_is_a_fresh_identity:" + ($mwx_id2 > $mwx_id1)
CLOSE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T10 / T11 -- DELETE FLAGS, IT DOES NOT REMOVE, AND THE HIGH-WATER MARK
* SURVIVES.
*
* THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO WORKSPACE PACK: allocation is max(WS_ID)+1 derived
* from the SURVIVING rows, so the deleted rows must keep being COUNTED or the
* next NEW would inherit a deleted workspace's durable identity. T10 reads
* the flagged row still on disk under SET DELETED OFF; T11 proves the next
* mint went UP rather than reusing the hole.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
WORKSPACE DELETE MWXONE

SET DELETED OFF
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR mwx_hw_after_delete = WS_ID
? "MWX_T10_deleted_rows_are_still_on_disk:" + ($mwx_hw_after_delete >= $mwx_id2)
CLOSE
SET DELETED ON

WORKSPACE NEW MWXONE
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR mwx_id3 = WS_ID
? "MWX_T11_delete_did_not_lower_the_high_water_mark:" + ($mwx_id3 > $mwx_id2)
CLOSE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T12 -- PURGE IS A RETAINED ALIAS AND STILL REACHES THE VERB.
*
* The verb was called PURGE until the owner read its output: "delete is a
* flag and that means the row still exists just ignored." In xBase the pair
* is exact -- DELETE flags, PACK removes -- so "purge" belongs on the PACK
* side and this verb is on the flag side. The alias survives so existing
* scripts keep working; this arm asserts that rather than assuming it.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
WORKSPACE PURGE MWXONE
WORKSPACE NEW MWXONE
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR mwx_id4 = WS_ID
? "MWX_T12_purge_alias_still_reaches_the_verb:" + ($mwx_id4 > $mwx_id3)
CLOSE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T13 -- DELETE REFUSES A WORKSPACE DECLARED IN THIS SESSION.
*
* Deleting a declared workspace's rows would leave it running with no durable
* identity. Retire it with DESTROY first. MWXONE is live right now, so the
* refusal is the expected path and the arm reads that its row SURVIVED.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE DELETE MWXONE
USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
? "MWX_T13_declared_workspace_kept_its_live_row:" + (WS_ID = $mwx_id4)
CLOSE

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 4 -- THE MINIDB CONTAINER.
*
* SAVE <name> MEMO stores a POSTURE: the tables stay on disk and the memo
* holds a description. SAVE <name> MEMO MINIDB stores a CONTAINER whose
* PAYLOAD IS THE DATABASE -- the table bytes ride along inside the memo.
*
* MINIDB IMPLIES V3, and the reason is mechanical rather than stylistic: the
* embedded posture must be SELF-LOCATING to survive being re-pointed at RAM,
* so it has to carry its own DBFROOT/IDXROOT rather than trusting whatever
* the environment says at load time.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXTWO
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXAS

* --- build a small database with a MEMO field, so the container is proven to
* --- carry more than fixed-width bytes -------------------------------------

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
WORKSPACE NEW MWXDB
WORKSPACE SWITCH MWXDB

SELECT 0
CREATE X64 MWXNOTE (ID N(4,0), TAG C(10), NOTES M)
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE TAG WITH "FIRST"
REPLACE NOTES WITH "carried through the container"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 2
REPLACE TAG WITH "SECOND"
REPLACE NOTES WITH "second row rides along too"

GO 1
? "MWX_G3_container_source_has_its_memo:" + (NOTES = "carried through the container")

WORKSPACE SAVE mwx_container MEMO MINIDB

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T14 -- PLAIN LOAD ... MEMO REFUSES A MINIDB PAYLOAD, BY DESIGN.
*
* A MINIDB's tables have no disk home. Standing up empty areas over missing
* files is the silent-success failure this codebase hunts, so the refusal is
* the feature. The arm reads that the CURRENT session survived the refusal --
* the answerable question -- rather than claiming anything about what was
* not loaded.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE LOAD mwx_container MEMO
SELECT MWXNOTE
GO 2
? "MWX_T14_session_survived_the_minidb_refusal:" + (ALLTRIM(TAG) = "SECOND")

WORKSPACE CLOSE

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T15 / T16 -- HYDRATION INTO RAM WITH ZERO DISK READS.
*
* LOAD <name> MEMO RAM mounts the container into the VDISK. The transcript
* reports "hydrated ... FROM THE MEMO: N file(s), N B ... (zero disk reads)"
* and an oracle byte-compare per file; those are EXTERNAL measurements and
* are not claimed by an arm (the IDXDIFF precedent). What the arms claim is
* that the ROWS ARE THERE and the MEMO SURVIVED THE ROUND TRIP.
*
* ADDRESSED BY NAME. After hydration the tables land at slots the posture
* does not control (R130), so SELECT MWXNOTE is the only spelling that can
* be trusted here.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mwx_container MEMO RAM
VDISK STATUS

SELECT MWXNOTE
GO 1
? "MWX_T15_row_one_hydrated_from_the_memo:" + (ALLTRIM(TAG) = "FIRST")
? "MWX_T16_memo_value_survived_the_container:" + (NOTES = "carried through the container")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T17 -- A HYDRATED TABLE IS WRITABLE.
*
* Hydration is not a read-only projection. The write lands in RAM and dies
* with the unmount, which is what makes a container a working set rather
* than an archive.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

REPLACE NOTES WITH "written after hydration"
? "MWX_T17_hydrated_table_is_writable:" + (NOTES = "written after hydration")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T18 -- WRITEBACK IS THE RETURN LEG (RAM/memo -> real disk).
*
* Enumeration comes from the POSTURE's AREA lines, never the session's attach
* order, and a shortfall ABORTS having written nothing. WITH INDEXES copies
* index container BYTES only -- LMDB is not carried (owner rule: lmdb only
* for disks) -- so a destination needing SET ORDER TO TAG would want
* BUILDLMDB first. This arm reads the row back from the REAL DISK copy after
* the RAM disk is gone, which is the only reading that proves the return leg
* rather than the mount.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mwx_container TO DBF/mwxback WITH INDEXES
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT
DO x64

SET PATH DBF DBF/mwxback
SELECT 0
USE MWXNOTE
GO 1
? "MWX_T18_writeback_reached_the_real_disk:" + (ALLTRIM(TAG) = "FIRST")
? "MWX_T19_writeback_carried_the_post_hydration_write:" + (NOTES = "written after hydration")
CLOSE

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 5 -- THE SHORTFALL CONTRACT.
*
* LOAD RESOLVES AND PROBES THE DECLARED MEMBERS BEFORE ANYTHING IS CLOSED, so
* a load that cannot be completed leaves the CURRENT session STANDING rather
* than destroying it and then reporting the wreckage. Until that landed, LOAD
* closed every area, failed every open, and still said "restored 0 area(s)".
*
* Indexes are NOT part of the check -- derived and rebuildable, and the choice
* travels in the posture -- which is why T22 exists separately.
* ===========================================================================

* THE POSTURE MUST DECLARE TWO MEMBERS, AND THE FIRST CUT DECLARED ONE.
* With a single member, removing it left PARTIAL nothing to restore -- so
* MWX_T21 read an UNRELATED table that happened to be open from earlier and
* reported .T. for a claim it never tested, and MWX_T22 addressed a table the
* posture had never contained. A SECOND, SURVIVING member is what makes both
* arms answerable: the load has something to fail on AND something to bring
* back.
*
* THIS IS A FALSE-GREEN SHAPE THE MORNING'S CHECK CANNOT CATCH. That check
* asks whether a green sits under a "no file open"; this one sat under a
* perfectly real read of a perfectly open table -- just not the table the
* operation under test had touched. A marker can lie by reading the WRONG
* LIVE THING, not only by reading nothing.

SET PATH DBF DBF/mwxback
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF AS MWXBACK

SELECT 1
CREATE X64 MWXKEEP (KPK C(4), KPLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE KPK WITH "P1"
REPLACE KPLBL WITH "SURVIVOR"

WORKSPACE SAVE mwx_shortfall MEMO V3
WORKSPACE CLOSE

* --- remove ONE declared member, leave the other, then ask for it back -----

SET PATH DBF DBF/mwxback
ERASE MWXNOTE CONFIRM
? "MWX_G4a_member_really_removed:" + (FILE("DBF/mwxback/MWXNOTE.dbf") = .F.)
? "MWX_G4b_survivor_still_on_disk:" + (FILE("DBF/mwxback/MWXKEEP.dbf") = .T.)

* --- stand up a KNOWN, HEALTHY session that the refusal must not disturb ---

DO x64
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
SELECT 0
USE MWXP
GO 1
? "MWX_G5_healthy_session_standing:" + (ALLTRIM(PLBL) = "ALPHA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T20 -- THE REFUSAL LEAVES THE SESSION STANDING.
*
* This is the arm the whole shortfall contract exists for. The posture
* declares a table that is gone; LOAD must abort having closed NOTHING, and
* the reading that proves it is that the UNRELATED open table is still
* exactly where it was.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE LOAD mwx_shortfall MEMO
SELECT MWXP
GO 1
? "MWX_T20_session_survived_the_shortfall_refusal:" + (ALLTRIM(PLBL) = "ALPHA_ONE")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T21 -- PARTIAL OPTS BACK INTO THE PERMISSIVE BEHAVIOUR EXPLICITLY.
*
* Same posture, same missing member, one keyword: the survivors come back and
* the absent one is REPORTED rather than silently skipped. The arm reads a
* SURVIVOR, because "the missing one is missing" is exactly the claim no
* marker in this language can make.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE LOAD mwx_shortfall MEMO PARTIAL
SELECT MWXKEEP
GO 1
? "MWX_T21_partial_restored_the_survivor:" + (ALLTRIM(KPLBL) = "SURVIVOR")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* MWX_T22 -- A MISSING INDEX DOES NOT REFUSE.
*
* Indexes are derived and rebuildable, so they are deliberately OUTSIDE the
* shortfall probe. Deleting the index directory and loading again must
* SUCCEED -- a load that refused here would make the posture brittle against
* a file the engine can regenerate.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/mwxback
ERASE DIR DBF/mwxback/indexes CONFIRM
WORKSPACE LOAD mwx_shortfall MEMO PARTIAL
SELECT MWXKEEP
GO 1
? "MWX_T22_missing_index_did_not_refuse_the_load:" + (ALLTRIM(KPLBL) = "SURVIVOR")

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 6 -- THE CATALOG IS A TABLE, AND IT SAYS WHAT IT IS.
*
* WORKSPACE CATALOG reports the memo catalog read-only. FMT is the PAYLOAD:
* DTSHEMA 2 and 3 carry a POSTURE, MINIDB 1 carries the TABLE BYTES. The two
* rows this script wrote should differ on exactly that field, which is the
* cheapest possible proof that the container and the posture are not the same
* artifact wearing two names.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE CATALOG

USE &_WORKSPACES\WORKSPACES.dbf
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "mwx_container"
? "MWX_T23_container_row_is_flagged_minidb:" + (AT("MINIDB", FMT) > 0)
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "mwx_shortfall"
? "MWX_T24_posture_row_is_flagged_dtshema:" + (AT("MINIDB", FMT) = 0)
CLOSE

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 7 -- TEARDOWN.
*
* MUST STAY ABOVE NOTHING: this is the end. Every workspace this script
* DECLARED is retired so the NAME has no live row and the next run MINTS
* rather than ADOPTS. The ROWS remain by design (D10.3) -- retirement is
* supersession, not deletion, and a destroyed workspace leaves a record.
*
* Fixtures are erased. The writeback directory goes too: it is scratch, and
* leaving it would make MWX_G4's "really removed" reading depend on whatever
* the previous run left behind.
* ===========================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXBACK
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXDB
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXKID
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXAS
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXTWO
WORKSPACE DESTROY MWXONE

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXA
ERASE MWXP CONFIRM
ERASE MWXSHARE CONFIRM
ERASE MWXK CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/MWXB
ERASE MWXQ CONFIRM
ERASE MWXSHARE CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
ERASE MWXNOTE CONFIRM
ERASE DIR DBF/mwxback CONFIRM

DO x64

FORMULA "MWX-SHAKEDOWN-END"

* ===========================================================================
* READ RULE, REPEATED AT THE BOTTOM BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE A READER STOPS.
*
*   37 markers must PRINT.
*     13 guards : MWX_G0a G0b G0c G0d G0e G1a G1b G1c G2 G3 G4a G4b G5
*     24 arms   : MWX_T1 .. MWX_T24
*   Derived, not asserted:  grep -c '^? "MWX_'  ->  37
*
* AND ONE WARNING THE COUNT CANNOT GIVE YOU. A marker can be backed by a
* real read of a real open table and still be worthless, if that table is
* not the one the operation under test touched. MWX_T21 did exactly that on
* the first run: PARTIAL restored NOTHING, and the arm read a table that had
* been open since an earlier section, reporting .T. for a claim it never
* tested. Checking that no green sits under a "no file open" does not catch
* it. When you read this file, ask of each arm not only "was something
* open" but "is that thing what the verb above was supposed to change".
*
* If the count is short, a marker ERRORED and printed nothing -- that is a
* FAILURE, not an omission, and the missing one is the interesting one.
* If any MWX_G* reads .F., every MWX_T* below it is UNPROVEN, not failing.
* ===========================================================================
